WEBVTT

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:04.840
NIST PQC standards use parameter sets and security categories to describe intended

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.795
strength levels and operational choices.

00:00:06.795 --> 00:00:07.580
At a high level,

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.425
a security category helps compare an algorithm parameter set to a target level of

00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:12.735
classical security strength.

00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:15.110
Practitioners do not need to perform the mathematics,

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:19.300
but they do need to understand that parameter choices affect both security posture and

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.440
system behavior.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.690
Parameter sets influence key sizes, ciphertext sizes, signature sizes, performance,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.171
memory use, bandwidth, and protocol overhead.

00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:30.185
Larger or stronger is not automatically better for every deployment.

00:00:30.185 --> 00:00:34.095
A high-volume service, a constrained device, a certificate-heavy workflow,

00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:36.720
and an offline signing process may all have different limits.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.120
A parameter choice that is acceptable in one environment may cause interoperability or

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.520
performance problems in another.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.080
This is why PQC migration requires testing with real protocols and realistic traffic.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.230
The impact is not only the algorithm benchmark.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.010
It is the behavior of the whole system: handshake size, packet fragmentation,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.685
certificate chain size, firmware image size, hardware capacity, latency, memory pressure,

00:00:57.685 --> 00:01:00.080
logging, monitoring, and failure handling.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.475
Small assumptions can become large deployment issues.

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:05.670
The practical approach is to match parameters to risk, protocol,

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.295
and operational constraints.

00:01:07.295 --> 00:01:11.110
Start with standards-based options, then test in the environments where they will run.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.890
Security teams should bring architects, developers, infrastructure owners, device teams,

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.960
and vendors into the conversation early.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.360
PQC is cryptographic, but its tradeoffs are deeply operational.
